        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "An increase in the partial pressure of carbon dioxide in arterial blood (PaCO2).",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Normal PaCO2values—35–45 mmHg.",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypercapnia is synonymous with hypercarbia.",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CO2—end product of aerobic cellular metabolism; considered the primary drive for ventilation by stimulation of central chemo receptors in the medulla oblongata; carried in the blood in three forms: bicarbonate (65%), bound to hemoglobin (30%), and dissolved in plasma (5%; the source of PaCO2values); constantly being added to alveolar gas from the pulmonary circulation and removed by alveolar ventilation.",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Uncommonly encountered in nonanesthe tized, clinically normal patient; result of alveolar hypoventilation.",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cardiovascular—may result in endogenous catecholamine release, which may induce cardiac arrhythmias; may cause vasodilation leading to hypotension.",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemic/lymphatic/immune—may alter acid-base balance; acute increase results in production of excess hydrogen ions and decrease in pH (respiratory acidosis).",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nervous—brain is primary affected organ; cerebral blood flow related to PaCO2in a linear fashion; hypercapnia results in increased cerebral blood flow and intracranial",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "pressure; PaCO2 >90 mmHg may lead to",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CO2narcosis and unconsciousness.",
              "pdf_page": 756,
              "printed_page": 674,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "pressure; PaCO2 >90 mmHg may lead to"
            }
          ]
        },
